WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:05.560
HUBBARD: Good morning everyone.
This is part of the Renewing America Series

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.690
on Risks and Priorities in Public Pension
Plans. And we have an absolutely wonderful

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:20.070
360 degree view of the problem or situation
on our panel with Brad Belt, who is the vice

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:27.949
chair of Orchard Global Asset Management;
a former CEO of the PBGC, Janet Cowell, who

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:34.620
is the state treasurer of North Carolina,
overseeing almost $80 billion in pension fund

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:42.640
investments for almost 900,000 public employees
and also chairs the state banking commission;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.629
and Scott Stringer, who's the comptroller
of the city of New York and a great friend

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:54.950
of Columbia University, thank you for that.
This topic is an extremely important one for,

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:00.629
I would argue, some obvious and some non-obvious
reasons. The obvious ones are familiar to

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:07.140
everyone. Public pensions lie at the intersection
of policy discussions about retirement security,

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.130
capital markets, and the health of municipal
finance.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.610
The unfunded obligations of some, if not many
plans, have been a concern to economists for

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:23.510
some time. There is an estimated $2.7 trillion
funding gap in the nation with an average

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:31.140
funding rate of under 78 percent. The Economist
magazine last year called Illinois "Greece."

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.280
But there's a less obvious and really interesting
reason to have this conversation today, that

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:43.430
these plans have been an important center
of discussion about ways to improve in a cost-effective

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:49.460
way, retirement security of millions of Americans.
So Janet, I'd like to start with you if I

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:55.640
might. When you and I were speaking earlier,
you talked about an absence, if you will,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:02.140
of a federal vision and leadership. What can
we learn from the variability across state

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.000
and local plans in terms of what works and
what doesn't? And in that regard, what's the

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.530
future of the traditional defined benefit
plan?

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.640
COWELL: Great. Well, thanks for having me
today.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.500
And yes, I don't think there's -- I'm not
expecting federal leadership on this issue

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.800
anytime soon: either branch, legislative or
executive. So, I do think there's a lot of

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.890
interesting work at the states, and there
is no one size fits all solution to this.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:32.360
North Carolina is a 95 percent funded plan.
It is -- has been that way. We have funded

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.780
every year since 1941 when the pension was
established, so good track record of accountability.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:46.650
So, I think in certain states, you will find
a long life for defined benefit. I will say

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:52.170
I get tired sometimes of folks coming to North
Carolina with the cookbook solutions, saying

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.160
that we should blow up the defined benefit
plan to fight the good funding and good track

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:04.120
record in our state, and simply for maybe
just purely philosophic or ideological reasons

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.870
go to a different system.
I will say I think there should -- there's

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:12.710
probably a core system that would work across
the country and in different variations, which

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:19.160
is some sort of hybrid choice where people
have a for-savings, centralized professional

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:24.590
management of funds, and then they could choose
a defined contribution or defined benefit.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:29.940
I will -- I will point to the UNC system,
which has had that very plan since 1973 and

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.970
it's worked very well.
So there are answers out there, and some good

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:36.550
examples that could be adopted around the
country.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:43.530
HUBBARD: So you supervise both DC and DB plans.
Now are those for different employees, or

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.800
like the UNC example, they're people who choose
between both and have both?

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:55.470
COWELL: Right now, we're -- the university
has a hybrid choice. We have a defined benefit

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:00.959
and then we have a series of supplemental
plans also run centralized through my office

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:06.500
of about $8.5 billion. We have a quarter million
people in those plans, and those are just

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:12.310
to supplement what is a fairly modest benefit
in North Carolina, the typical pension for

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:17.870
a retiree is around $22,000. They get Social
Security, but that's supplemental, gets them

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.470
to a more liveable, healthy retirement.
HUBBARD: OK.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:29.020
Scott, you've certainly championed defined
benefit plans in New York. How will we trade

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:34.539
off the protection for employees that you've
stressed with cost to taxpayers and honest

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:38.400
accounting for these costs?
STRINGER: Well I -- let me -- let's start

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.069
off and say to the Council on Foreign Relations,
I think this is a terrific opportunity to

00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:49.479
have a important conversation about our pension
system, defined benefit, and how we move forward.

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:55.610
And I always am happy when I can share a platform
with Janet Cowell, who has been very thoughtful

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.090
about these issues on a national level.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.449
But I -- I think for those of you in this
room who came of age during the city's fiscal

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:08.999
crisis, New York is somewhat unique. Because
we've learned in a sense from our past mistakes.

00:05:08.999 --> 00:05:15.389
If you think going back to 1975 when accounting
was done, budgeting was done, literally on

00:05:15.389 --> 00:05:21.539
a napkin. After the budget was done, they
filed away the paper napkins and thought that

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:24.279
we could spend money without any accountability.

00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:30.720
So after the fiscal crisis, transparency was
put in place. We had a state monitor. We still

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.819
do today to a certain extent. And we had to
account to the rating agencies. So the city

00:05:35.819 --> 00:05:44.550
budgeting process and the pension system now
are scrutinized in a way that is very rare

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.099
among other states and other cities.

00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:53.550
And so for me, the defined benefit plan is
about making sure that we continue to put

00:05:53.550 --> 00:06:00.580
in place transparency efforts to continue
to make sure that we get to a fully-funded

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:08.089
position. In New York City, our pension system
is funded on average to 72 percent. We've

00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:12.879
exceeded our actuarial target for the last
ten years. That's the good news. We all know

00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:17.789
that that fluctuates. And I do think we've
been able to put procedures in place now that

00:06:17.789 --> 00:06:23.080
I'm going to�talk about today that I think
will show that New York could be a model for

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.089
the kind of national reform we're talking
about.

00:06:25.089 --> 00:06:28.580
HUBBARD: Let me draw you out on that. What
-- what are some of those procedures? And

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:33.520
I know you've been a champion of transparency.
What are ways in which the city has moved

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.369
toward a more transparent reporting?

00:06:35.369 --> 00:06:40.520
STRINGER: You know, the job of city comptroller
is interesting. Because in addition to being

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.090
a fiduciary for a $160 billion fund -- it's
the fourth largest in the United States, 12th

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:51.770
largest in the world -- I'm also the auditor-in-chief.
And we actually look at city agencies. We

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:56.649
root out waste and fraud. So before you look
at what other people are doing, it's important

00:06:56.649 --> 00:06:58.469
to get your own house in order.

00:06:58.469 --> 00:07:03.129
So when I became comptroller, we issued a
six-point ethics plan. First thing we did

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:06.960
was finally ban placement agents. We got rid
of the middlemen, who did little work but

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.719
got a lot of fees, and we finally pushed them
out of the pension system. The second thing

00:07:11.719 --> 00:07:18.479
we did, believe it or not, is we hired a risk
officer. A compliance officer is being hired.

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:24.879
We brought in a national manager of our pension
system, Scott Evans, who we were able to lure

00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:30.039
out of retirement and now he is our chief
investment officer.

00:07:30.039 --> 00:07:35.869
Part of what we're doing is getting our own
house in order. More transparency. More of

00:07:35.869 --> 00:07:40.379
our employees now are doing more of their
own disclosure. Because part of what we want

00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:46.180
to show our managers, as we hold them to a
higher standard, we want them to know that

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:52.179
we also believe in transparency. So we came
in and did this on day one. We didn't wait

00:07:52.179 --> 00:07:55.440
a year or two. And I think that goes a long
way to the work we're doing.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.249
I'll tell you something else we're doing that
I think is significant. We did not wait when

00:07:59.249 --> 00:08:05.620
it came time to implement GASB 67 and 68.
We could have waited on GASB 68 for another

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.620
year but we --

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:07.620
(CROSSTALK)

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.039
HUBBARD: On the off chance the audience isn't
intimately familiar with the Government Accounting

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:12.490
Standards Board, tell them what 67 and 68
are.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:18.979
STRINGER: Well, this is essentially new transparency
measures that follow the GAP accounting principles.

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:23.800
This is very important for New York City,
especially after the city fiscal crisis. And

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:30.569
we implemented those changes, which basically
ensures that we can look at our liabilities

00:08:30.569 --> 00:08:36.039
in real time. And we didn't wait. Because
I thought it was important now, working with

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:41.199
our trustees. And let me tell you, we worked
with our�fifty-eight trustees -- union members

00:08:41.199 --> 00:08:47.709
-- who are not afraid of transparency or scrutiny
-- and we put it out there. And I think that

00:08:47.709 --> 00:08:52.190
ensures that our bond underwriters, that people
who look at our pension system, get a sense

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:57.100
that we mean business and that we want the
world to understand that as we move forward,

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:01.870
it's with an eye towards everybody working
together and having a real discussion on retirement

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:02.870
security.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:07.990
HUBBARD: Well, Brad, you've certainly had
a lot of experience with both success and

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:10.970
failures of traditional pension plans and
the many hats you've worn --

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.250
BELT: I can take credit for the failures,
not for the successes.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:19.810
HUBBARD: OK. All right. Do you see the rise
in importance of defined contribution plans

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:26.790
continuing? Or do you see that defined benefit
plans offer superior transaction costs or

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.430
investment philosophies that make them a key
part of the solution? And is there a middle

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:36.040
ground in this debate? As Janet noted, this
has sometimes been polarized that defined

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:39.510
contribution is good, defined benefit bad,
or vice versa. How do you see it?

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.779
BELT: Yeah, it's become a highly-politicized
debate. And before I answer that, I also just

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:47.820
want to thank Richard and staff of CFR for
putting this program together. It's not often

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.290
that public pension or pension-related issues
are fodder for breakfast discussion.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:57.800
And it's also -- it's also a delight to share
the dais with Scott and Janet. Janet has been

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:02.030
a thought leader on these issues nationally.
And Scott, I commend you and your staff for

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:06.470
what you've done over time, really, in the
area of transparency.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:10.970
It was -- he recently retired, which I think
is a great loss. But your longtime chief actuary,

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.339
Bob North, was really --
(CROSSTALK)

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:16.520
BELT: -- one of the ones that pushed for kind
of broad discussion of liabilities, providing

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.250
more information to different stakeholders.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:24.500
You're absolutely right that the debate has
become far too politicized, Glenn. My own

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:32.270
personal view is that defined benefit plans
as we know them today are suboptimal. They

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:38.089
-- from the standpoint of both the provider
and the employee, the user. From the provider's

00:10:38.089 --> 00:10:43.330
standpoint, they're managing a whole host
of risks that they're not really best positioned

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:48.750
to deal with; whether it's investment risk,
longevity risk, operational risk. From the

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:54.490
employee's standpoint, it's not a portable
benefit. And I think that's one of the principal

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:57.600
issues that creates some job locks sometimes.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.320
But on the other hand, defined contribution
plan are not a panacea, either. For any number

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:07.850
of reasons, but not the least of which is
they shift all the risk to the individual.

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:14.209
Now, the idea of defined benefit plans of
pooling risk is a great thing. Cost efficiency

00:11:14.209 --> 00:11:18.879
is a good thing. But in defined contribution
plans, the individual bears all of those.

00:11:18.879 --> 00:11:23.970
And I think the evidence is pretty compelling
that individuals are not best positioned to

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:29.180
manage investment risk. They're not in a position
to manage their own longevity risk. So that's

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:34.959
why we need to come to some type of hybrid
structure that marries the best elements of

00:11:34.959 --> 00:11:35.959
both.

00:11:35.959 --> 00:11:39.060
And there are some plans that are -- you're
seeing some models developing at the state

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:43.910
level. Unfortunately, not at the national
level. The only leadership you get at the

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:49.760
national level is at the margins; improving
defined contribution plans, auto-escalation,

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:54.470
auto-enrollment, those kinds of things. But
we really need to get to a point where we're

00:11:54.470 --> 00:12:00.110
coming up with a structure that will ensure
greater retirement security for all Americans.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:04.230
To Janet's point about the absence of leadership,
when I was in the Bush Administration heading

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:10.079
the PBGC, we'd sit down -- and Glenn knows
how the White House apparatus works with CEA

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:14.260
and National Economic Council. But you had
national retirement security policy being

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:21.320
set by Department of Labor and EBSA, Social
Security Administration, PBC, PBGC, Center

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:27.790
for Medicare and Medicaid, and the IRS. No
real coordination. And that's at the national

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:32.379
level, let alone at what's happening at the
state level. And that needs to change for

00:12:32.379 --> 00:12:33.379
the good of the country.

00:12:33.379 --> 00:12:37.290
HUBBARD: Let me ask you a couple of follow-ups
on that. You talked about portability. Give

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:44.189
an example for the audience. So think about
let's say a teacher in a typical defined benefit

00:12:44.189 --> 00:12:48.699
plan. Walk me through her career and portability
issues.

00:12:48.699 --> 00:12:53.639
BELT: Well, the -- one thing to actually note
is in the defined -- in the defined benefits

00:12:53.639 --> 00:12:58.620
system, in the public sector, there's a -- there's
a lot more penetration. Probably 90 percent

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:04.430
of public sector employees belong to a defined
benefit plan. It's dramatically less than

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:08.970
that in the private sector. And the number
in the private sector has been going way down

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:14.329
as pension plans in the private sector are
increasingly frozen. That's not the case in

00:13:14.329 --> 00:13:16.930
the defined -- in the public sector.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:23.180
The issue is you've locked in -- your benefits
may not vest for a period of time. And then

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:28.800
your ramp-up -- your benefits are based typically
on your final average salary over a long period

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:34.529
of time. And if there are early retirement
subsidies, you're incented, certainly in the

00:13:34.529 --> 00:13:40.400
public sector, also somewhat in the private
sector, to stay with one employer for a long

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:45.580
period of time. Now, that may be a good thing.
And it's -- defined benefit plans have often

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:52.040
been viewed as great human capital management
tools, both for recruitment and retention

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:58.139
and managing the exit of the workforce. But
that creates its own problems. Sometimes it's

00:13:58.139 --> 00:14:04.490
more difficult to move -- you know, to change
the -- the structure of your workforce if

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:10.200
everybody's saying, 'I need to earn that last
few years of benefit under the defined benefit

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.200
plan'.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:14.650
HUBBARD: Let me ask you one other thing. Because
you mentioned some experiments that are going

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:19.379
on. Can you talk about a particular state
or example that you think is interesting?

00:14:19.379 --> 00:14:23.700
BELT: Well, Janet's probably more attuned
to what's happening at the state level. There

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:30.779
are a number of states that I think are models
for -- and cities -- for hybrid structures

00:14:30.779 --> 00:14:36.300
in terms of the benefit structure. But also,
I think critically important is corporate

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:43.069
governance. And there -- you know, the issue
at the end of the day, the biggest problem

00:14:43.069 --> 00:14:47.379
as I see it in the public sector -- and Janet
and Scott may have a different view on this,

00:14:47.379 --> 00:14:53.970
ultimately about the funded status of the
pension plan and the ability of a municipality

00:14:53.970 --> 00:15:01.019
or state to meet its future obligations is,
how well that plan is governed. What are the

00:15:01.019 --> 00:15:09.300
incentives to encourage regular contributions
into the plan, to manage the assets and liabilities?

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:13.410
Most pension plans in the public sector -- and
I think, you know, people will trot out numbers,

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:18.029
Glenn, about whether it's a $4 trillion problem
or a $1 trillion problem. And it's probably

00:15:18.029 --> 00:15:25.290
somewhere in between -- is most public sector
systems are going to be able to meet their

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:28.779
obligations. That's not true of all of them.
And Illinois would be an example of that.

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:32.620
But that's not true of all the municipalities
on a go-forward basis.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:38.290
But the qualification to that is to make sure
you don't have assets moving in the wrong

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:44.080
direction, that they're prudently managing
the investment risk, that they're not increasing

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:49.850
liabilities along the way, and they're consistently
making contributions and not playing games

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:54.820
through all the actuarial calculations that
often occur. If all of those things happen,

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.279
then it's a manageable problem.

00:15:56.279 --> 00:16:00.790
HUBBARD: OK. I have a question for each of
you. Because this topic of portability as

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:06.040
has come up a lot. And it is the nub of differences
between defined contribution fund benefit

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:12.160
plans. What are ways that one could improve
the portability of a traditional defined benefit

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:17.089
plan, either on it own or as part of a mix?
Scott?And we can work our way down.

00:16:17.089 --> 00:16:22.680
STRINGER: Well, I think this is part of the
national conversation that we have to have.

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:29.510
And I agree with Janet that whether it's portability
or what kind of system works for more Americans,

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:35.410
I think we have to have in some respects a
nonpartisan, honest discussion about that.

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:41.300
This is probably so far the rational discourse
on this issue. You have dyed-in-the-wool democrats

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:46.550
with a Bush official all recognizing that
we are concerned about liability. But we recognize

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:51.760
the value of defined pension plan, we recognize
that retirement security is critical to the

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:57.970
wellbeing of this country, and that many people
can't manage the 401(k), which is laden with

00:16:57.970 --> 00:16:59.370
fees and things like that.

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:03.529
So what I would say -- and I'll just take
one issue as it relates to portability or

00:17:03.529 --> 00:17:10.780
job -- or job switching at different points
in a career. There are people who aspire to

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:15.470
working in government. It's really not a dirty
word. Whether you're a firefighter or police

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:20.270
officer or government official, there is a
sense that you want to do this kind of work.

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:24.520
And you recognize early on that you're not
doing this work because you're going to make

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:29.410
a lot of money. In fact, you're going to sacrifice
a lot of earning power to serve the public.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:35.940
At the end of the day, we sort of say to our
officials, the people who protect us, you

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:40.460
will make less money at the front end, but
you at the back end will be able to take care

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:44.670
of yourself and your family later on. The
average pension that we pay out in New York

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:51.130
City is less than $40,000 a year. It's important
to know that. These are not big payouts. $40,000

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:55.320
to live in this city is a struggle, at best.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:18:01.150
So I think we have to look at ways to make
the workforce -- to protect people in this

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:06.020
issue. Portability is certainly critical to
that. But I would argue that we first have

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:14.590
to recognize that people go into this work,
not because of financial reasons or even because

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:19.540
of the pension path, but because they love
what they do. And we need them to love what

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:20.540
they do.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:25.300
COWELL: Well, and I'll just give you a concrete
example just of this portability issue. Because

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:32.300
my mother was a public schoolteacher and my
dad was a Methodist minister which, as those

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:38.550
of you who know Methodists, we move a lot.
And so Mom taught in Kentucky and Colorado

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:47.190
and Tennessee and Utah. And when my dad recently
passed away, I was going through Mom's finances.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:53.840
And despite the fact -- she now had several
defined benefit checks coming in. None of

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:59.570
them large. But, you know, I was very grateful
for that defined benefit plan that both my

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:04.430
parents had through the Methodist church and
the public school system and the sort of,

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:08.980
you know, middle class income that it's giving
my mother.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:14.970
So there are challenges. But I would say it
still works in the current system. This is

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:19.700
where the federal piece is important. If you
really want portability, you've got to have

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:24.080
some federal participation in this. And to
me, it would almost be like a Chinese menu

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:28.170
options for states. You know, those states
that have the governance structures or the

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:32.590
discipline or the local governments, like
Scott's, to support a defined benefit, you

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:37.040
certainly can do that. You could maybe have
more portability between the defined benefit.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:41.650
But you could also have in your Chinese menu
of options, you know, these defined contributions

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:49.440
plans and an ability to transfer those funds
easily across states and across plans if you

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:54.280
had some sort of framework within -- where
you had some partnerships and understanding.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:59.580
HUBBARD: So from the portability on the public
side, do you see it as more as just earlier

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:06.211
vesting, to use the example of perhaps your
mother's experience? Or do you see it as a

00:20:06.211 --> 00:20:10.870
multiemployer kind of plan, perhaps with federal
leadership? And what's the future here?

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:14.790
COWELL: Well, I certainly think centralization
is the way to go. And North Carolina is another

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:20.440
example of that. I run all the local plans.
I run the state plan for teachers and state

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:24.810
employees, which is why we're large despite
the fact that we're, you know, not as -- so

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:29.800
I think centralization. You can't create the
level of expertise to run these plans fifteen

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:34.330
times in a given state. You just don't have
the money or the -- or the talent pools to

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:35.330
do that.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:40.740
So trying to get to more centralized structures,
but then having agreements between those structures

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:41.740
for more transferability.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:43.320
STRINGER: Can I also just make --

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:44.650
HUBBARD: Of course.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:49.680
STRINGER: That we -- just to put a thought
in people's head, that there's a lot of discussion

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:54.020
about portability and how we can keep people
in the public pension system, give them options.

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:55.760
I know Janet's thought a lot about this.

00:20:55.760 --> 00:21:00.820
But there's another phenomenon that I think
people have to recognize if we want to continue

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:05.660
to have talent within our public employee
system. A lot of folks in the private sector

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:10.210
are actually stuck in the private sector.
We've got a lot of kids who run up huge college

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:15.810
tabs and a lot of graduate school finances
where they then get a job on Wall Street or

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:20.050
law firms. And they're the ones who are actually
stuck in the system, because they're paying

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:27.030
off huge tuition fees. They want ultimately
to shift from the private sector, where they're

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:31.910
making money, but then they want to do good
work. I talk to young people all the time,

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:38.690
and they can't wait to finish off paying off
their loans in twenty years or thirty years

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:44.450
and then get into government or do something
in the public interest. And I just think we

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:47.710
have to spend a little more time thinking
about these issues.

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:51.740
Part of the frustration with Washington is
that no one is thinking about any of these

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:56.660
issues. And that's why I think over the last
few years we've seen state attorney generals,

00:21:56.660 --> 00:22:04.360
treasurers, and comptrollers with a real opportunity
to create new ideas and thought that we can

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:09.640
work on that will push Washington. Because
it's not coming from the top down to us. We

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.130
have to spur this discussion from the bottom
up.

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:18.230
BELT: Glenn, I would just note that it's -- it's
one thing to look in the rear-view mirror.

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:24.710
But looking ahead is, what's the structure,
the benefit structure, that's going to be

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:29.900
optimal for employers going forward, as well
as employees going forward?

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:34.870
Most young people that I -- I've hired a lot
of young people entering the workforce. They

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:41.260
have no expectation, frankly no desire out
of the box to chart a career path with their

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:44.850
first job; that they're going to stay -- whether
it's in the public sector or in the private

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:51.471
sector. And the data's pretty compelling that
on average most employees, most workers, change

00:22:51.471 --> 00:22:56.500
jobs every five to seven years throughout
their life span. Now, some may stay in the

00:22:56.500 --> 00:23:00.980
public sector or in a particular job in the
public sector throughout their lives. I'll

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:05.250
-- that's another debate as to whether that's
a good thing or not.

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:10.831
But the fact is, most people don't attach
a lot of value coming out of the box, out

00:23:10.831 --> 00:23:15.740
of school, to a traditional defined benefit
plan. And if they are moving from one employer

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:20.360
to another, they're -- they may not be vesting
benefits. They may be leaving money on the

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:26.490
table. And the 401(k) option is the -- actually,
as suboptimal as it is, it's the only thing

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.020
that's generating retirement savings for them.

00:23:29.020 --> 00:23:33.460
So that's why it's so critically important
that we do come up with a structure that is

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:38.581
going to require some centralization. It's
essentially a multiemployer construct. Whether

00:23:38.581 --> 00:23:43.600
that's architected or governed at the federal
level or not or at some compact amongst the

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.860
states I think is an open discussion.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:52.890
That's a complex issue in and of itself, who
actually is responsible for those liabilities,

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:57.910
both management of the centralization, making
investment decisions, as well as ultimately

00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:01.680
who's on the hook for those liabilities, are
things that we need to talk about, we need

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:06.860
to have a conversation about. Because right
now, we're on a path, because of the erosion

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:11.050
of the defined benefit system in the private
sector, because of the public -- the funding

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:16.270
issues in the public sector, as well as all
the risk-shifting that's occurring in the

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:21.570
defined contribution or 401(k) sector, we're
not on a good path right now. And I think

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:25.970
the retirement security of future generations
of Americans is going to be imperiled unless

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:28.230
we all sit down and talk about these issues
collectively.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:34.210
HUBBARD: Let me ask all of you a somewhat
technical question, but very important, about

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:40.340
funding. So 95 percent funding or 72 percent
funding or the national average is 78 percent

00:24:40.340 --> 00:24:44.780
funding. Depends of course on what you choose
as a discount rate. So let me ask each of

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:50.230
you, what is the way in which we should be
discounting pension obligations?

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:52.030
(CROSSTALK)

00:24:52.030 --> 00:25:00.530
HUBBARD: I can tell economics is a favorite
subject of our audience. I can see it in their

00:25:00.530 --> 00:25:01.530
faces.

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:02.530
COWELL: Yeah.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:05.610
BELT: Well, I'll -- I'll jump in on that one,
since we -- I was recently on the Society

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:09.780
of Actuaries Blue Ribbon commission, where
we looked very long and hard at that issue.

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:16.100
And that's why I alluded to whether the public
sector problem is a $1 trillion problem or

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:23.480
a $4 trillion problem really depends on what
you're using as your mortality table assumptions,

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:29.240
your discount rate, your other smoothing mechanisms,
actuarial levels -- levers.

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:39.570
My own view is that at least for comparability
purposes and for being able to understand

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:46.100
what the cost of the obligation is on a riskless
basis, that there should be disclosure of

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:50.960
the liability, both in the private sector
and public sector on a risk-free basis, on

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:52.750
a Treasury rate discount basis.

00:25:52.750 --> 00:25:58.600
Now, having said that, that's often conflated
with driving or dictating the funding policy

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:03.080
and the investments policy, which I don't
agree with. I think each jurisdiction should

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:09.410
be able to, you know, assume its own risks,
as long as stakeholders have the relevant

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:12.620
set of information available to them.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:19.510
What I think is a concern is in the public
sector right now, when Scott says he's whatever

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:27.570
percent funded and Janet says she's 95 percent
funded, I really don't know as a taxpayer

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:34.220
or as a stakeholder in this system what that
means. Because you can literally change your

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:38.660
funded status from one day to the next if
you changed your discount rate assumption.

00:26:38.660 --> 00:26:42.990
And indeed, if you say well, we're going to
take a little bit more risk this year, that

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:49.080
has the effect, under the public sector system,
of making the size of the liability smaller.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:54.530
HUBBARD: So your -- so your bottom-line recommendation
would be just transparency, let's report a

00:26:54.530 --> 00:27:00.720
seven percent or whatever rate the plan is
using, but side by side the ten-year treasury?

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:02.040
Just to be concrete -- that's what you're
recommending?

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:03.950
BELT: And that was a recommendation --

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:04.950
HUBBARD: OK.

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:09.620
BELT: -- of the -- of the Blue Ribbon Commission.
And it's one -- again, so people understand

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:10.980
that there is risk in the system.

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:11.980
HUBBARD: Yes.

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:15.540
BELT: If you wanted to fease this liability
-- and nobody's suggesting you do that -- at

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:22.170
this very costly rate, here's what it would
entail. But it also allows comparability --

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:23.170
HUBBARD: Yeah.

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:24.720
BELT: -- across plans and across systems

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:25.720
HUBBARD: Janet?

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:29.810
COWELL: Yeah. And I agree. I think the comparability
is important. So, I mean, we've been supportive

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:35.560
of some of the initiatives to have the standardized
discount rate. And, you know, we -- we can

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:36.560
--

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:38.240
HUBBARD: What do you disclose as a discount
rate?

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.570
COWELL: Our -- our target is seven and a quarter.

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:41.570
HUBBARD: OK.

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:45.870
COWELL: And obviously, there's multiple accounting
assumptions -- that you could try to standardize

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:49.790
all of them. There are -- that gets a little
harder --

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:50.790
(CROSSTALK)

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:54.281
COWELL: -- because you do have very different
portfolio constructions. But I certainly support

00:27:54.281 --> 00:27:59.120
this move towards a national standard, apples-to-apples
comparison, to the extent possible.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:00.120
HUBBARD: OK. Scott?

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:05.280
STRINGER: We -- we brought down our discount
rate from 8 to 7 percent in recent years.

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:11.100
I think it's two things. I think we have to
look at consistency and transparency. So part

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:16.050
of our disclosure documents show that the
risk-free discount rate is actually 2 percent.

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:23.590
So we want people to understand that we recognize
what a true number is. The 7 percent that

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:28.350
we now operate under, our actuarial target,
we've actually hit that target for the last

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:32.450
ten years. Doesn't mean we'll do it next year.
But that is sort of our track record. And

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:36.610
because we're longterm investors, we have
to look at it on that basis.

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:41.361
One of the things I think that New York City
and other states are doing well, the ones

00:28:41.361 --> 00:28:46.480
that are doing this, is that when I talk about
consistency, we pay into our pension fund

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:51.580
when there's a shortfall, no matter what.
New Jersey doesn't do it. Detroit doesn't

00:28:51.580 --> 00:28:54.220
do it.
One of the things that's made, I think, our

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:58.940
system strong is our consistency. People know
at the end of the day, we are going to make

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:04.980
up any shortfall. The city puts money into
our pension system, no matter what. So we

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:10.360
don't get into an underfunded situation that
could trigger a default or something more

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:15.450
critical. And I think states and localities
who have pension investments have to be prepared

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:20.880
to be consistent by continuing to make those
payments in bad times as a way of keeping

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:21.880
the system strong.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:26.480
BELT: Although, you know, I -- I think it's
an interesting -- it's almost a tautological

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:32.550
issue. I often hear from my friends in -- in
the public sector that we've hit our return

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:39.510
target over the last ten or twenty years.
And I'm going -- but -- so you should be well

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:45.930
overfunded, but -- but they're not. Even on
the discount -- the rate of return discount

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:51.500
rate assumption, still only 78 percent funded,
that gets to the point that Janet was making;

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:56.581
that there's actually a lot that's going on
underneath just the rate of return on the

00:29:56.581 --> 00:30:02.060
asset side. That means either liabilities
of (inaudible) benefits have been increased

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:08.190
over time or contributions haven't been made
consistently or there's been something going

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:13.490
on with the amortization or smoothing periods
or you have new mortality tables that have

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:20.620
been implemented. It's just -- people focus
too much on the return on assets and not all

00:30:20.620 --> 00:30:21.760
the other component parts.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:22.760
STRINGER: I agree with you.

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:23.760
HUBBARD: Right.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:24.760
STRINGER: I agree with you.

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:27.820
HUBBARD:...only actuaries think living longer
is a crisis. But I'll put that aside.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:28.820
(LAUGHTER)

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:32.360
One thing the Council on Foreign Relations
doesn't discount is views of members. So we're

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:37.050
going to open up now for questions. So if
you have a question, please raise your hand.

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:43.600
Wait for the mic. Say your name and affiliation
and question for the panel. The floor's open.

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:45.190
Alan, right there. Patricof.

00:30:45.190 --> 00:30:50.130
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) ...question because I'm
not knowledgeable about portability. You mentioned

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:55.810
your mother going from -- and you were asked,
everyone was asked and no one actually explained

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:59.680
it. What happened to your mother when she
went from New Mexico to Utah to whatever --

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:00.680
COWELL: Yeah. Yeah.

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:01.680
QUESTION: -- in terms of her pensions?

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:06.140
COWELL: Well, so, usually vesting periods
are around five years to even --

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:07.140
QUESTION: Right.

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:11.120
COWELL: -- vest. And so Kentucky, we didn't
live there five years, so she had no Kentucky

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:16.830
benefits. When we went to Colorado and Utah
and Tennessee, we lived there five years so

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:22.130
she actually did vest. Now, each state is
also different in terms of is it -- like in

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:25.830
North Carolina when you hit twenty years,
you've kind of hit the jackpot, you get an

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:29.890
actually decent benefit. Before that, it's
not that great.

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:36.270
So that's where I think, again, you could
have a more standardized federal policy or

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:44.770
guidance on more of a pro rata, and rewards
for shorter-term employees, as opposed to

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:49.430
this 'we're just going to encourage the twenty-
or thirty-year employee and it differs state

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:50.430
by state'.

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:53.690
HUBBARD: Yeah. Well, when people look at high
pensions in the public sector, they're often

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:58.500
looking at a long-tenured public sector worker.
But Janet's right. For most people, if they

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:02.450
were to leave early, they would get nothing
like the numbers that you see.

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:04.920
Yes, ma'am. Right here.

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:11.770
QUESTION: Thank you. Hi, I'm Nili Gilbert
of Matarin Capital Management, cofounder and

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:13.210
portfolio manager.

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:18.980
I was wondering if you could share your views
on how the governance of pension plans affects

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:24.650
the success in outcomes. On the one hand,
Treasurer Cowell, you're the sole trustee

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:29.980
currently of the North Carolina plans, but
I know thinking about that structure. At the

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:36.110
other extreme, Comptroller Stringer, you've
got, what, five boards and fifty-eight trustees.

00:32:36.110 --> 00:32:42.330
And then somewhere in the middle, I think
PBGC has three trustees; right? The secretaries

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:43.611
of commerce, treasury and --

00:32:43.611 --> 00:32:44.611
(CROSSTALK)

00:32:44.611 --> 00:32:45.611
QUESTION: -- labor --

00:32:45.611 --> 00:32:46.611
(UNKNOWN): Yeah.

00:32:46.611 --> 00:32:51.010
QUESTION: -- and then a strong advisory board.
So we see such different governance structures

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:53.150
in this -- in this space.

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:57.300
To the extent that governance may affect the
success of outcomes, how should we think about

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:03.820
what's working and what could be adjusted?
STRINGER: Well, I actually think it's an excellent

00:33:03.820 --> 00:33:08.610
question. You know, in the case of the New
York City pension fund, we do have five boards,

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:12.240
fifty-eight trustees. You know, some look
at that and say well, that's like herding

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:16.800
cats; right? I mean, how do you -- how do
you quickly invest, how are you nimble, how

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:22.710
do you -- how do you deal with a very quick
market, and how do you make sound investments?

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:28.120
And part of our challenge is going back to
the boards time and time again, basically

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:29.840
doing the same thing over and over again.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:36.250
One of the things that I'm wrestling with
is how we begin reforming the pension system

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:40.740
in New York City. One, does it require state
legislation? We're looking into that. Two,

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:46.880
it definitely requires the ability to work
with our pension -- with our pension trustees,

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:52.460
folks who represent our unions, to come up
with a system, one of consolidation and one

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:56.230
of alignment so that we can meet the challenges
of the 21st century.

00:33:56.230 --> 00:34:00.960
New York City is unique in this. But, again,
we're a $160 billion pension fund. We are

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:05.680
going to be working towards that this year.
Want to work with the mayor. Want to work

00:34:05.680 --> 00:34:12.050
with our labor allies. Because this is something
that I think is fundamental to increase the

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:16.899
ability for us to get that rate of return
to -- to make sure that we can do the work

00:34:16.899 --> 00:34:17.899
we have to do.

00:34:17.899 --> 00:34:21.590
The second thing that we want to do as it
relates to pension reform is we want to make

00:34:21.590 --> 00:34:27.570
sure that we hold our managers to the highest
standards, as well. I started the conversation

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:33.000
talking about the reform that we have undergone
within our bureau of asset management. But

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:37.870
we also want to make sure that if we're paying
managers fees, we're also getting the best

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:42.320
performance; that we have benchmarks, that
we have opportunity, that we also hire diverse

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:48.500
managers. And these are the issues that we're
grappling with as we set the stage for looking

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:52.030
at ways we can better align the New York City
pension system.

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:58.240
COWELL: Just a second. I do think governance
is critical. There's no one -- just like in

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:05.770
the design of these plans, no -- no one solution.
The huge benefit of the sole fiduciary is

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:13.020
the accountability. So there is one neck to
-- to cut off. And the legislature -- and

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:17.260
I think part of the reason North Carolina
has been so consistent in funding, just maybe

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:25.200
-- was because the treasurer, who is elected,
has so much going on -- you know, riding on

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:26.910
whether or not they fund it.

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:31.070
I will say the challenge -- and I have looked
at the governance structure of -- probably

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:36.660
the ideal is -- you know, it's the middle
bowl of oatmeal. I'm, you, know maybe the

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:41.950
small bowl. You're the large bowl. And then
the middle is that expert panel of, you know,

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:47.200
five or six people who all have professional
expertise in investing. And that's -- that's

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:53.020
the holy grail. I will say that is very difficult
to get to because of the conflicts of interest.

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:58.010
And I will say more recently with the Securities
and Exchange Commission regulations that now

00:35:58.010 --> 00:36:05.190
put anyone who has fiduciary responsibilities
in these public plans under SEC rules -- you

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:10.800
know, when people understand that, do governors
or House speakers want to be subjected to

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:15.580
those rules and give up all of those financial
contributions? So I will say that is going

00:36:15.580 --> 00:36:21.060
to be a huge disincentive -- in North Carolina,
specifically -- for them to go to a board

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:25.160
structure, because there are so many other
regulatory strings attached.

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:27.000
HUBBARD: Any thoughts or --

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:31.960
BELT: Well, the only one I -- the only point
I would make in addition to all of that is

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:36.680
that you have an agency problem, more in the
public sector than you do in the private sector,

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:43.860
is that legislators set the contribution policy.
And their incentives are all too often to

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:49.060
use less of current tax revenues to put into
the pension plan, particularly during periods

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:53.740
of budgetary stress. Some of the -- and I'm
not sure you call it a governance structure.

00:36:53.740 --> 00:36:59.440
But I think the state that's been consistently
the best funded, Wisconsin, the state of Wisconsin

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:05.180
Investment Board, actually has things pretty
much on autopilot in items of the contributions

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:10.130
that have to go into the system. There's less
latitude on the part of the legislators to

00:37:10.130 --> 00:37:14.160
underfund the contribution in any given year.
It's one reason why it's very well-funded.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:16.450
STRINGER: But let me also stress --

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:17.450
HUBBARD: Yeah.

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:21.780
STRINGER: -- that here in New York City is
an example of us making that contribution.

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:27.560
When you look at Chris Christie in New Jersey,
their trouble is because they don't fund.

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:33.070
They divert the money. Part of the challenge
of New York City -- and Janet alluded to it

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:37.750
with the State legislature and with other
elected officials that have to be involved

00:37:37.750 --> 00:37:44.030
in reforming our own system -- we have to
be very strategic in how we approach pension

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:45.030
alignment.

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:49.520
During my campaign for comptroller, when I
was debating Eliot Spitzer, I remember during

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:53.460
one of our debates he slammed his fist down
on the table and said, when I'm comptroller,

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:59.550
we are going to have pension alignment and
pension reform. And I -- when it came to me,

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:06.690
I didn't slam my fist down. I gently put my
hand down and said, we have to work with fifty-eight

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:11.780
trustees. We have to build consensus. We have
to go to Albany. It is going to be a difficult

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:12.780
journey.

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:19.110
But if we can get there in New York City,
then I think we set the stage for a lot of

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:24.980
the conversation that has to happen on a national
level; that we can be nimble, that we can

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:30.190
actually have a coherent conversation with
all the different players to get to where

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:35.200
we have to be to protect the retirement security,
in my case, of 700,000 retirees.

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:38.000
HUBBARD: Other questions? John?

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:45.060
QUESTION: I'm John Biggs, a former chairman
of TIAA-CREF. We were a pension system. And

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:50.900
today I think it's about $600 billion. And
about half of that is public funds and half

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:52.620
of it is private.

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:55.290
HUBBARD: You have my money.

00:38:55.290 --> 00:39:00.080
QUESTION: (inaudible) your money. I take it
very seriously.

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:01.080
(CROSSTALK)

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:06.350
QUESTION: It's my retirement money, "take
good care of it" was our slogan for many years.

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:12.730
We comply with ERISA, because we are a major
player in the private sector. We're also a

00:39:12.730 --> 00:39:19.440
defined contribution plan primarily. There
are some small DB plans in the system. I've

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:25.040
often thought that if ERISA were applicable
to the public sector, the problems would go

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:30.514
away. The funding would be mandatory. There
wouldn't be any messing around. There wouldn't

00:39:30.514 --> 00:39:36.260
be any actuaries playing games with the numbers.
Which, in my opinion -- I'm an actuary, as

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:42.920
well -- has been a real scandal to the actuarial
profession. So the numbers aren't trusted

00:39:42.920 --> 00:39:47.300
by the -- by the community because of that.

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:51.950
GASB -- originally, I was involved with the
appointment of the people in the GASB. We

00:39:51.950 --> 00:39:56.450
didn't get very good people to come in. I
think it has much improved. It's much better

00:39:56.450 --> 00:40:03.220
now and the standards are stronger. There's
a movement right now to -- among a number

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:11.001
of states -- to consider mandatory simple
plans with sort of an IRA model. And a big

00:40:11.001 --> 00:40:18.080
issue is whether ERISA will be applicable
to those. And the state's position is we won't

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:21.270
put in the plan if an ERISA is applicable
to them.

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:30.580
ERISA has a lot of important employee protections,
which are violated routinely in the public

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:39.470
sector. I think it's probably unlikely that
we're ever going to have ERISA applying to

00:40:39.470 --> 00:40:46.450
it. But the standards -- and I think probably
a major reason for the disappearance of the

00:40:46.450 --> 00:40:54.930
DB plan among companies who are required to
comply with ERISA and PBGC and have PBGC protection,

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:59.060
there's no PBGC protection for the state employees.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:08.730
But could we move toward a more -- I think
a federal effort to apply ERISA? It was -- they

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:13.650
were constitutional issues, but they were
not totally clear. They've been the same ones

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:21.430
with Social Security. But with ERISA's standards,
I think you would not have the kind of funding

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:24.740
Mickey Mouse that we all see going on.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:30.640
COWELL: Well, and -- I like your suggestion.
And I will tell you, I was in the schools

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:37.830
in North Carolina and realized that we had
school districts offering eighteen different

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:45.210
defined contribution supplemental plans. There
was no vetting. There was no oversight. And

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:48.810
sometimes these people were picked because
they were somebody's golfing buddy; right?

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:54.870
And we're telling our teachers who are working
for our kids that you're putting your money

00:41:54.870 --> 00:42:00.290
in this -- and sometimes, it was a 5 percent
upfront fee, 5 percent backend fee. I mean,

00:42:00.290 --> 00:42:02.760
they were outrageous fees.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:08.421
The fact that we don't protect our employees
better than that, what we did -- well, this

00:42:08.421 --> 00:42:15.140
has a happy TIAA-CREF ending -- is that we
went to a centralized model and said you can

00:42:15.140 --> 00:42:20.830
offer those plans, but we're going to offer
a centralized 403(b) for teachers with oversight.

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:27.800
You know, bid out low fees, high quality.
We ended up going with TIAA-CREF. So that

00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:29.500
was a well-placed question.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:31.980
HUBBARRD: This was not a --

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:32.980
(CROSSTALK)

00:42:32.980 --> 00:42:35.650
HUBBARD: Any other thoughts from the panel
on ERISA?

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:36.650
(CROSSTALK)

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:42.940
BELT: Well, I would just note that, John,
while I would agree that the ERISA funding

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:48.900
rules are better than they are in the public
sector, and certainly being -- been improved

00:42:48.900 --> 00:42:55.610
further post-Pension Protection Act in 2006,
I would not offer quite the ringing endorsement

00:42:55.610 --> 00:43:00.580
of ERISA that you did. I think there are a
lot of issues still with ERISA.

00:43:00.580 --> 00:43:06.020
And even as it covers private sector pension
plans, the fact of the matter is PBGC's taken

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:12.040
over several thousand very underfunded pension
plans, not withstanding the application of

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:17.119
those funding rules. In many cases, the company
ended up being less than 50 percent funded.

00:43:17.119 --> 00:43:23.220
So the rules aren't quite as tight as they
might be. Although, I suspect that many corporate

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:26.830
sponsors would like them to be even more relaxed
than they are.

00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:30.000
HUBBARD: Other questions? Sir.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:35.100
QUESTION: Thank you. Scott Swid from SLS Capital.

00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:40.640
Given the value cycle that Comptroller Stringer
mentioned, how public employees works for

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:46.890
maybe below the compensation of the corporate
sector but then gets rewarded with a DB pension

00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:54.591
that's good; given financial crisis, bankruptcy,
some sort of out-of-court work out, where

00:43:54.591 --> 00:44:00.590
should that commitment sit and get treated?
Should it have super protections like some

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:07.119
legislatures have given it? What are your
views on -- on that in a work out?

00:44:07.119 --> 00:44:15.570
COWELL: Well, I mean, the courts are, of course,
working through these issues. What I've observed

00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:20.270
-- I mean, for COLAs, you know, the guaranteed
cost of living adjustment, that's maybe the

00:44:20.270 --> 00:44:26.760
first thing that goes. And then the rest is
the negotiation. And dire situations, of trying

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:32.360
to find the right balance between, you know,
bondholders in Detroit and the pensioners.

00:44:32.360 --> 00:44:36.430
But, you know, I can't tell you what the exact
formula is.

00:44:36.430 --> 00:44:42.930
Of course, in municipal -- and with the bankruptcies,
a lot more flexibility than at the state level.

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:48.869
I mean, in North Carolina we have a system
where we preempt all of that, because we control

00:44:48.869 --> 00:44:53.869
all the local municipal finance. So if anybody's
ever getting close to some sort of bankruptcy,

00:44:53.869 --> 00:45:00.150
we intervene and I actually run the checking
account for that city. So, you know, trying

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:03.410
to get to those kind of systems where you
don't get to the point where you're actually

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:05.670
having to work it out in a bankruptcy court.

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:10.540
STRINGER: And I agree with Janet. I mean,
part of -- part of our responsibility, our

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:14.869
fiduciary responsibility, is not to get to
this point. You know, ironically, we spend

00:45:14.869 --> 00:45:22.911
more time talking about pensions being sacrosanct
as it relates to bankruptcies, when we really

00:45:22.911 --> 00:45:26.650
should be talking about making sure we don't
get to this point.

00:45:26.650 --> 00:45:31.790
And that is why the way New York City now
does its budgeting after the fiscal crisis

00:45:31.790 --> 00:45:37.850
is we actually look at the budget plan four
times a year. We do a budget analysis almost

00:45:37.850 --> 00:45:42.960
every quarter. We make adjustments in mid
year. The mayor modifies the budget. The council

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:48.610
looks at it. I analyze it. And we're constantly
monitoring our pension fund, and we're also

00:45:48.610 --> 00:45:53.730
monitoring the way we do our budgeting. After
we're done within our city process, we then

00:45:53.730 --> 00:45:59.220
have to go to the state and we make a case
to the Financial Control Board, which is still

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:03.720
in effect. And then we meet with the rating
agencies. So the mayor and I are constantly

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:06.640
looking at this budget so we don't get to
that point.

00:46:06.640 --> 00:46:10.390
And I think the model -- although New York
City, I'm not trying to make us better than

00:46:10.390 --> 00:46:16.610
anywhere else -- there has to be more attention
paid on the front end so that we don't have

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:21.280
this incredible discussion on the back end.
Sometimes I feel -- and I want to be honest

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:28.600
about this -- that when a crisis does ensue,
the first thing some look at is to take away

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:35.290
the defined benefit pension that somebody,
one, has contributed a lifetime to or a lot

00:46:35.290 --> 00:46:41.150
of -- many years. Sometimes that pension's
$25,000 a year. But we keep talking about

00:46:41.150 --> 00:46:45.730
that as the solution to getting out of the
bankruptcy. And I just think we have to move

00:46:45.730 --> 00:46:52.400
this conversation very early into how we're
going to govern the way we do budgeting in

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:54.500
our cities and in our states.

00:46:54.500 --> 00:46:56.010
HUBBARD: Anything, Brad?

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:59.600
BELT: The courts -- as Janet noted, the courts
will work through the applicability of the

00:46:59.600 --> 00:47:05.150
chapter nine in -- in the bankruptcy context
for municipalities going forward.

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:12.640
I think it circles back to where we started,
though. If creditors to municipalities thought

00:47:12.640 --> 00:47:18.221
that they were senior, they may recognize
that they're not necessarily senior, they're

00:47:18.221 --> 00:47:23.960
subordinate to the pension obligation in at
least certain jurisdictions. That's why it's

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:28.940
so important that there's greater transparency.
You have more information about the size of

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:32.790
that nut that you're subordinate to on a go-forward
basis.

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:36.609
HUBBARD: Bob Hormats, I think you had a follow-up.

00:47:36.609 --> 00:47:41.400
QUESTION: Yes. (OFF-MIKE) ...worked in government
for four years and the State Department. So

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:46.810
I'm familiar with some of the issues that
were raised about government employees making

00:47:46.810 --> 00:47:51.420
relatively little and then making more or
at least having a comfortable retirement.

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:56.330
I would like to go back to a point that was
made early in the conversation about the role

00:47:56.330 --> 00:48:01.250
of the Federal government. And at this point,
we obviously can't come to one decision as

00:48:01.250 --> 00:48:08.140
to an overall, all-encompassing plan. But
what process -- if you were to try to sort

00:48:08.140 --> 00:48:12.530
out with all these agencies that are involved
and all the states and all the sub-entities

00:48:12.530 --> 00:48:19.280
in the states, how would the Federal government
proceed in trying to develop an orderly process

00:48:19.280 --> 00:48:25.690
for adjusting this problem, for addressing
these various aspects of the problem?

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:32.580
Because orderly decision-making, as Glenn
knows very well -- and Brad and I have worked

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:38.650
on many of these issues -- orderly decision-making
is not really a hallmark of the way Washington

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:44.520
works on complicated issues. And I'd be very
curious as to what recommendations the panel

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:50.540
has on what the government, if it decided
that this was a problem, needed to be adjusted

00:48:50.540 --> 00:48:57.060
on a more comprehensive basis with some consistency
among various entities and our federal system

00:48:57.060 --> 00:49:02.210
-- how would the government go about addressing
this? How -- what would be the process for

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:08.950
getting to a rational, mutually-consistent
-- not perfect among all states, but at least

00:49:08.950 --> 00:49:14.010
mutually-consistent set of guidelines for
moving forward?

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:15.980
COWELL: How about Brad --

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:16.980
(CROSSTALK)

00:49:16.980 --> 00:49:20.980
BELT: Well, Bob, one thing that I've suggested
in the past and written about is the need

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:27.230
to establish at the federal level, for lack
of a better term, a national retirement securities

00:49:27.230 --> 00:49:33.930
czar or office of requirement security policy
within the executive branch. That nominally

00:49:33.930 --> 00:49:39.630
falls within the purview of the National Economic
Council right now. But I think the issues

00:49:39.630 --> 00:49:45.360
are too important. And the -- and the NEC
is not I think constituted to really address

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:49.750
those issues optimally. We've both dealt with
those organizations.

00:49:49.750 --> 00:49:57.020
So if you had an office of national security
policy whose sole mandate was to coordinate

00:49:57.020 --> 00:50:01.840
at least at the national or federal level
retirement security policy against all the

00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:07.970
different agencies and executive branch entities
that touch upon requirement security policy,

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:09.840
that would be a starting point.

00:50:09.840 --> 00:50:16.210
STRINGER: Maybe this is simple. But one of
the best meetings I've ever attended in my

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:23.330
time in government was a meeting months ago
at the White House. Our -- our office is looking

00:50:23.330 --> 00:50:28.450
at ways to engage more managers of color.
People of all different backgrounds should

00:50:28.450 --> 00:50:34.030
have the opportunity to manage money in our
pension fund. Most of the investors, quite

00:50:34.030 --> 00:50:41.080
frankly, look like me, though they wear better
suits. And we need to have a more impactful

00:50:41.080 --> 00:50:46.260
way to engage people in small funds with different
diverse backgrounds. Studies show that when

00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:49.790
different people get to manage our pension
funds, we do better.

00:50:49.790 --> 00:50:54.869
So the fact that I was able to go to the White
House and sit with SecretaryJarrett -- Labor

00:50:54.869 --> 00:51:01.020
Secretary Valerie Jarrett to just have a discussion
about how we do this with other people around

00:51:01.020 --> 00:51:07.030
the country was just so important. It's a
meeting that took six years in the making.

00:51:07.030 --> 00:51:12.680
But it certainly energized the conversation.
You know, whoever gets elected president,

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:17.600
whoever controls Congress, sometimes just
bringing people from different states and

00:51:17.600 --> 00:51:22.619
different people from different points in
life we can have a profound impact.

00:51:22.619 --> 00:51:27.690
I think Janet would agree -- because she said
this -- it is very frustrating that on a city

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:33.960
or state level we grapple with this with no
expectation that we can have these meetings

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:39.440
on retirement security. And that's so unfortunate.
And that's why conversations like this are

00:51:39.440 --> 00:51:43.750
worth having among us. Because all of you
are thought leaders. We've got to talk to

00:51:43.750 --> 00:51:47.440
our different allies and say let's break it
up and let's come together.

00:51:47.440 --> 00:51:51.220
HUBBARD: Yeah.Bob, I would actually agree
with what Brad said; that you need some sort

00:51:51.220 --> 00:51:56.200
of central official. In the old Reagan/George
H.W. Bush Administration, there was an economic

00:51:56.200 --> 00:52:01.490
policy council chaired by the Secretary of
the Treasury. And inside that was a working

00:52:01.490 --> 00:52:06.040
group on this. And having that kind of structure
I think helps. Because this doesn't naturally

00:52:06.040 --> 00:52:09.670
emerge, as you know, (inaudible) process.

00:52:09.670 --> 00:52:10.880
HUBBARD: Sir.

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:18.740
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE). Hello. Yes. Thank you
very much. Mahesh Kotecha. I recently -- well,

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:28.040
not so recently listened to Robert Merton
who, you know, was asked what can management

00:52:28.040 --> 00:52:35.940
- you know, financial know-how - do for -- for
society? He was asked in light of the -- the

00:52:35.940 --> 00:52:41.140
financial crisis and all of that.
His answer was, let's create a method to empower

00:52:41.140 --> 00:52:49.240
the pensioner to address the very complex
issue, which is, I want a terminal retirement

00:52:49.240 --> 00:52:56.180
income that is targeted, that's based on my
willingness to work and save. And how do I

00:52:56.180 --> 00:53:01.880
choose among all these pension schemes and
investment alternatives that report on stuff

00:53:01.880 --> 00:53:06.480
that I really don't get an answer on as to
what I care about?

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:12.040
I'd like to really ask what is being done
to empower the pensioner to select among the

00:53:12.040 --> 00:53:17.690
myriad of complex plans that they have. And
I don't hear much about that. I think the

00:53:17.690 --> 00:53:23.800
technicalities go to the program, the plans
and the investment returns and the like and

00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:28.109
the actual assumptions. But really, from the
point of view of the consumer, the pensioner,

00:53:28.109 --> 00:53:31.690
what are we doing to empower them to make
choices that are really pertinent?

00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:36.340
COWELL: Yeah. Well, I can tell you, because
I run both the health plan and the pension

00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:41.140
plan in North Carolina, and those are very
important questions. Because as you move to

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:47.480
potential hybrid choice systems and get into
more discretion on the part of the pensioner

00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:55.280
or the health plan consumer, the whole literacy
and education and marketing is critical. And

00:53:55.280 --> 00:54:00.200
that's -- I will -- it is incredibly difficult.
And I will say in the public sector -- you

00:54:00.200 --> 00:54:05.650
know, Scott may agree here. It's very difficult
to get the marketing expertise, the communication

00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:08.830
expertise, the dollars to keep up with that.

00:54:08.830 --> 00:54:14.231
And I see people even making mistakes in their
selections with the basic defined benefit

00:54:14.231 --> 00:54:19.540
plans. I mean, I'll have a janitor come in
from some rural county who got bad advice

00:54:19.540 --> 00:54:24.980
from an HR representative and didn't, you
know, select the right beneficiary. And you

00:54:24.980 --> 00:54:28.550
see the consequences of these, even on a very
simple system.

00:54:28.550 --> 00:54:36.710
So whatever solution we come to, I think you've
got to design it where there are limited choices

00:54:36.710 --> 00:54:43.810
and some very good, you know, marketing selection
language. None of that is simple. I think

00:54:43.810 --> 00:54:48.000
the retirement is easier, maybe, than the
health plan, which I think we're seeing with

00:54:48.000 --> 00:54:52.290
the national health exchange and others. That's
only one -- that's one facet of the difficulty

00:54:52.290 --> 00:54:54.510
of these plans. But it's a very important
conversation.

00:54:54.510 --> 00:55:01.030
STRINGER: And I will tell you that as I move
around the city, I'm constantly having people

00:55:01.030 --> 00:55:07.160
who are about to retire or who may, in fact,
be in retirement come up to me with a million

00:55:07.160 --> 00:55:11.630
questions. And they're very concerned. They're
concerned about their pensions. They have

00:55:11.630 --> 00:55:17.200
a lot of questions. We spend a lot of time
in our community affairs unit actually fielding

00:55:17.200 --> 00:55:22.610
(inaudible) retiree or potential retirees.
We just -- the mayor's just now negotiated

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:30.700
72 percent of our labor contracts. And I cannot
tell you how many people are trying to follow

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:33.350
what those contracts mean when you go into
retirement.

00:55:33.350 --> 00:55:37.440
So we do have -- as reelected officials, we
really do have an obligation to do a very

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:42.720
good job explaining to people what their options
are, when should they retire, when should

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:47.220
they take money out, what is the different
aspects of this. Because, you know, a mistake

00:55:47.220 --> 00:55:51.570
can really have real impact, both in terms
of health benefits or pension -- pension opportunity.

00:55:51.570 --> 00:55:56.599
BELT: I would just note, it's a little bit
different on the private sector side. And

00:55:56.599 --> 00:56:01.560
I alluded to it with John, kind of the infirmities
or at least oddities of ERISA on the private

00:56:01.560 --> 00:56:08.369
sector side, is that historically in a defined
benefit plan you had the sponsor making every

00:56:08.369 --> 00:56:13.970
decision on behalf of the employee. And that
was entirely consistent with their fiduciary

00:56:13.970 --> 00:56:21.610
responsibility under ERISA. Yet, on the defined
contribution side, it was put out a menu of

00:56:21.610 --> 00:56:28.180
options but provide no advice and counsel
because of concern about fiduciary liability.

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:30.970
It's tough to square that circle. It makes
no sense.

00:56:30.970 --> 00:56:36.100
Now, post-PPA and some other legislation,
you're providing some safe harbor, so you're

00:56:36.100 --> 00:56:41.060
starting to get a little bit more information.
But at the end of the day, if the defined

00:56:41.060 --> 00:56:46.940
benefit plan sponsor of a company thinks that
this structure, these fees, these managers

00:56:46.940 --> 00:56:52.010
are the best in the defined benefit context,
then they should be able to offer that same

00:56:52.010 --> 00:56:57.780
option as a default option in defined contribution
context so you could take advantage of that

00:56:57.780 --> 00:57:02.380
professional management, scale efficiencies,
and everything else. But that hasn't been

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:05.420
the way ERISA's been read over the -- until
now.

00:57:05.420 --> 00:57:11.540
HUBBARD: Time for one last quick question.
Sir, right there.

00:57:11.540 --> 00:57:21.470
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) It's on. Ron Shelp, author
and filmmaker.

00:57:21.470 --> 00:57:27.570
If I could go back to something that Mr. Stringer
said, but it's indirectly related. Let's say

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:33.440
you're a young college student. You're out
of school. You've got a job. It's in the private

00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:38.230
sector. But you're not on Wall Street, you
never had those aspirations. But you do have

00:57:38.230 --> 00:57:46.130
an aspiration to have a middle class future,
sort of like your parents had, maybe. But

00:57:46.130 --> 00:57:51.690
when you look at the salaries they make, even
if they're good salaries, they're almost never

00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:53.510
with defined benefits plans.

00:57:53.510 --> 00:58:00.190
My son is in that case. He's been told to
have his IRA, it'll be matched. I personally

00:58:00.190 --> 00:58:07.540
don't see how over a long career, even if
he changed jobs numerous times, you can possibly

00:58:07.540 --> 00:58:13.670
save enough money in an IRA, especially with
inflation and other things coming on, to have

00:58:13.670 --> 00:58:16.820
a decent retirement. Any comments, please?

00:58:16.820 --> 00:58:23.590
STRINGER: I think that -- that is -- that
is part of the larger conversation about retirement

00:58:23.590 --> 00:58:31.660
security. And it's not just about union workers.
But rather, we should perhaps open up a retirement

00:58:31.660 --> 00:58:35.230
security to more people to aspire to the middle
class.

00:58:35.230 --> 00:58:42.560
Look, back in the '60s and '70s, a middle
class family would have one wage earner,you

00:58:42.560 --> 00:58:48.520
could be a typist, work in manufacturing,
and you could pay the bills and raise your

00:58:48.520 --> 00:58:52.530
family and you knew you were going to have
a small pension at the end of the day to take

00:58:52.530 --> 00:58:56.990
care of yourself in your old age.
Today, that's getting much more difficult

00:58:56.990 --> 00:59:03.260
to aspire to. Here's a challenge in our city.
We talk a lot about raising the minimum wage.

00:59:03.260 --> 00:59:09.140
I believe we should. But we can't stop there.
We have a high-tech economy that is booming

00:59:09.140 --> 00:59:15.240
in this city. We have got to prepare our kids
for that opportunity, which means more science,

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:20.849
more arts education. And, if we're going rival
Silicon Valley -- we've surpassed Boston -- these

00:59:20.849 --> 00:59:25.270
are the next generation of middle class jobs
- the coder jobs, the programmer jobs - that

00:59:25.270 --> 00:59:29.820
will allow our kids to do what their parents
and grandparents have done, which is move

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:33.340
up the ladder economically in this city.

00:59:33.340 --> 00:59:37.550
And while I do think there has to be a layer
of retirement security to that -- and there

00:59:37.550 --> 00:59:42.910
has to be a broader conversation about that
-- one of the failings about the city right

00:59:42.910 --> 00:59:49.330
now is we are not laying the foundation to
take advantage of these incredible job opportunities

00:59:49.330 --> 00:59:54.349
and companies that are now in this city and
are telling me -- telling me we do not have

00:59:54.349 --> 00:59:58.830
the ability to hire local kids in our public
school system because they don't have the

00:59:58.830 --> 01:00:01.270
skills, they're not getting the training.

01:00:01.270 --> 01:00:06.720
And the way we protect families in the future
is by educating these kids today. These are

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:11.520
the future fathers and mothers, and we've
got to make sure they have an opportunity

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:17.271
to do what we did and what our grandparents
did. We may be limited in terms of what we

01:00:17.271 --> 01:00:22.590
accomplish at this moment in our lives. But
we all think our kids have the potential to

01:00:22.590 --> 01:00:27.320
do anything they want. Especially for me,
I'm fifty-four and I have a three-year-old

01:00:27.320 --> 01:00:33.109
and a two-year-old. I was up at 3:00 in the
morning, by the way, before coming here. And

01:00:33.109 --> 01:00:39.480
I worry about what kind of economy is going
to be left for them, retirement security included.

01:00:39.480 --> 01:00:46.160
COWELL: I would say that politically, there's
a lot more emphasis right now on current cash

01:00:46.160 --> 01:00:51.100
in hand and wages. I mean, I never thought
that public employees in North Carolina would

01:00:51.100 --> 01:00:57.130
argue against funding a pension. But I found
out when it came to 'do I get a raise today

01:00:57.130 --> 01:01:02.400
or do you fund my pension', they'll take the
raise today. So that's a reason why I think

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:06.990
this pension issue will not be dealt with
in the immediate term. There's no huge incentive

01:01:06.990 --> 01:01:13.500
to do it. I do think it's a ticking time bomb
for my generation who do not have the savings

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:14.500
--

01:01:14.500 --> 01:01:15.500
(CROSSTALK)

01:01:15.500 --> 01:01:18.140
COWELL: -- do not have a plan -- our generation.
Ours.

01:01:18.140 --> 01:01:19.650
(CROSSTALK)

01:01:19.650 --> 01:01:28.200
COWELL: Ours. So it's -- it's ticking, but
the crisis isn't there yet. And then in the

01:01:28.200 --> 01:01:32.359
meantime, you've got twenty-year-olds, you
know, with high unemployment, low wage growth

01:01:32.359 --> 01:01:36.150
for middle class. And that's going be the
more urgent, immediate issue.

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:40.350
BELT: And I would simply close on since this
is Council on Foreign Relations, we talked

01:01:40.350 --> 01:01:45.180
about different models and structures. There's
actually a lot to look at abroad in items

01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:49.340
of being better educated, whether it's some
of the Scandinavian systems, the superannuation

01:01:49.340 --> 01:01:54.420
system in Australia, and others that we might
learn from, and it doesn't have to be invented

01:01:54.420 --> 01:02:00.050
here. So the CFR on a go-forward basis can
do some research and maybe get back to us

01:02:00.050 --> 01:02:04.420
about what some of these models might be in
their applicability here in the U.S. to help

01:02:04.420 --> 01:02:05.420
solve those problems.

01:02:05.420 --> 01:02:09.000
HUBBARD: May our guests never have a pension
closeout. But we're going to have to close

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:10.430
out. So thank you. And join me in thanking
the panel.

